A minimal TypeScript file (using the .ts extension):

function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

add(2, 3);      // fine
add('2', 3);    // TypeScript ERROR, caught before running anything

Compiling this file (npm install -D typescript, then npx tsc app.ts) produces plain app.js:

function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

Notice the compiled output contains zero trace of the type annotations at all — they exist purely to be checked during compilation, then are stripped away entirely, since JavaScript itself has no concept of types at runtime. In a real project, this compilation step is usually automated by your build tooling (the same Vite/bundler tooling from Module 6 and Module 10, lesson 5) rather than run manually via npx tsc for every file.

